/** @mainpage Qore tar Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section tarintro Introduction

    The \c tar module provides comprehensive TAR archive functionality for Qore, including:

    - Creating, reading, and modifying TAR archives
    - Multiple compression methods: gzip, bzip2, xz, zstd, lz4
    - Multiple TAR formats: USTAR, PAX, GNU, V7
    - Include/exclude glob filters and path stripping for extraction
    - Progress callbacks for long-running operations
    - Streaming API for large file operations
    - Stream-based constructors for piping archives through I/O streams
    - Support for symbolic links, hard links, and special files
    - In-memory archive operations
    - Archive encryption (AES-256-CBC) and multi-volume split/join
    - Archive integrity verification
    - Data provider module with 17 registered actions
    - \c qtar command-line tool

    The module is built on top of libarchive, providing robust and efficient archive handling.

    To use this module, use \c "%requires tar" in your code.

    @section tarquickstart Quick Start

    @subsection tarcreate Creating a TAR Archive

    @code{.py}
#!/usr/bin/qore

%requires tar

# Create a new uncompressed archive
TarFile tar("archive.tar", "w");
tar.add("readme.txt", "Hello, World!");
tar.addFile("document.pdf", "/path/to/document.pdf");
tar.addDirectory("images/");
tar.addFile("images/logo.png", "/path/to/logo.png");
tar.close();

# Create a gzip-compressed archive
TarFile tarGz("archive.tar.gz", "w", <TarCreateOptions>{"compression_method": TAR_CM_GZIP});
tarGz.add("data.txt", "Compressed content");
tarGz.close();
    @endcode

    @subsection tarread Reading a TAR Archive

    @code{.py}
TarFile tar("archive.tar", "r");
foreach hash<Qore::Tar::TarEntryInfo> entry in (tar.entries()) {
    printf("Entry: %s, Size: %d bytes, Type: %s\n", entry.name, entry.size, entry.type);
    if (!entry.is_directory) {
        binary data = tar.read(entry.name);
    }
}
tar.close();
    @endcode

    @subsection tarextract Extracting a TAR Archive

    @code{.py}
TarFile tar("archive.tar.gz", "r");
tar.extractAll(<TarExtractOptions>{
    "destination": "/destination/path",
    "overwrite": True,
    "preserve_permissions": True,
    "preserve_times": True,
});
tar.close();

# Extract a single file
TarFile tar2("archive.tar", "r");
tar2.extractTo("readme.txt", "/path/to/extracted/readme.txt");
tar2.close();
    @endcode

    @subsection tarfilters Extraction Filters and Path Stripping

    @code{.py}
TarFile tar("archive.tar.gz", "r");

# Extract only .txt files, excluding anything in tmp/
list<string> extracted = tar.extractAll(<TarExtractOptions>{
    "destination": "/output",
    "include": ("*.txt",),
    "exclude": ("tmp/*",),
});
tar.close();
printf("Extracted %d files\n", extracted.size());

# Strip leading path components (e.g., "project/src/main.c" -> "src/main.c")
TarFile tar2("project.tar", "r");
tar2.extractAll(<TarExtractOptions>{
    "destination": "/output",
    "strip_count": 1,
});
tar2.close();
    @endcode

    Glob wildcards supported by include/exclude filters:
    - \c * matches any characters (including \c /)
    - \c ? matches any single character
    - \c [abc] matches one character from the set

    @subsection tarprogress Progress Callbacks

    @code{.py}
TarFile tar("large-archive.tar.gz", "r");
list<string> extracted = tar.extractAll(
    <TarExtractOptions>{"destination": "/output"},
    sub (string name, int current, int total) {
        printf("[%d/%d] %s\n", current, total, name);
    },
);
tar.close();
    @endcode

    The callback receives \c (string entry_name, int current, int total) after each
    entry is extracted. If the callback throws an exception, extraction is aborted cleanly.

    @subsection tarmemory In-Memory Archives

    @code{.py}
# Create an in-memory archive
TarFile tar();
tar.add("data.json", make_json({"key": "value"}));
binary archive_data = tar.toData();
# archive_data can be sent over network, stored in DB, etc.

# Read from binary data
TarFile readTar(archive_data);
string content = readTar.readText("data.json");
readTar.close();

# Create compressed in-memory archive
TarFile compressedTar(<TarCreateOptions>{"compression_method": TAR_CM_GZIP});
compressedTar.add("file.txt", "Compressed content");
binary compressedData = compressedTar.toData();
    @endcode

    @subsection tarsymlinks Working with Symlinks and Hardlinks

    @code{.py}
TarFile tar("links.tar", "w", <TarCreateOptions>{"format": TAR_FORMAT_PAX});
tar.add("original.txt", "Original file content");
tar.addSymlink("symlink.txt", "original.txt");
tar.addHardlink("hardlink.txt", "original.txt");
tar.close();

# Reading link information
TarFile readTar("links.tar", "r");
hash<Qore::Tar::TarEntryInfo> entry = readTar.getEntry("symlink.txt");
if (entry.is_symlink) {
    printf("Symlink points to: %s\n", entry.link_target);
}
readTar.close();
    @endcode

    @subsection tarstreaming Streaming API

    @code{.py}
# Streaming write for large files
TarFile tar("large.tar", "w");
TarOutputStream os = tar.getOutputStream("bigfile.bin");
# Write data in chunks
os.write(chunk1);
os.write(chunk2);
os.write(chunk3);
os.close();
tar.close();

# Streaming read for large files
TarFile readTar("large.tar", "r");
TarInputStream is = readTar.getInputStream("bigfile.bin");
while (*binary chunk = is.read(4096)) {
    # Process chunk
}
readTar.close();
    @endcode

    @subsection tarstreamctors Stream-Based Constructors

    TarFile can be constructed from Qore InputStream and OutputStream objects for
    piping archive data through streams:

    @code{.py}
# Write archive to an OutputStream
FileOutputStream fos("archive.tar.gz");
TarFile tar(fos, <TarCreateOptions>{"compression_method": TAR_CM_GZIP});
tar.add("file.txt", "content");
tar.close();

# Read archive from an InputStream
FileInputStream fis("archive.tar.gz");
TarFile tar2(fis);
list<hash<TarEntryInfo>> entries = tar2.entries();
tar2.close();
    @endcode

    @note Operations that re-read the archive (e.g., calling entries() then read()) will
    buffer the stream into memory on first use, since streams are not seekable. For large
    archives where memory is a concern, use the file-path constructor instead.

    @section tarcompression Compression Methods

    The module supports multiple compression methods:

    |!Constant|!Extension|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_CM_NONE|.tar|No compression (plain tar)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_CM_GZIP|.tar.gz, .tgz|Gzip compression (most common)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_CM_BZIP2|.tar.bz2|Bzip2 compression (better ratio, slower)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_CM_XZ|.tar.xz|XZ/LZMA2 compression (best ratio, slowest)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_CM_ZSTD|.tar.zst|Zstandard compression (fast, good ratio)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_CM_LZ4|.tar.lz4|LZ4 compression (fastest, lower ratio)

    Compression levels range from 1 (fastest) to 9 (best):

    |!Constant|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_COMPRESSION_DEFAULT|Default compression level
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_COMPRESSION_FASTEST|Fastest compression (level 1)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_COMPRESSION_BEST|Best compression (level 9)

    @section tarformats TAR Formats

    The module supports multiple TAR formats:

    |!Constant|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_USTAR|POSIX.1-1988 ustar format (100-char filename limit)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_PAX|POSIX.1-2001 pax format (recommended, no practical limits)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_GNU|GNU tar format (extended attributes support)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_V7|Original V7 Unix tar format (legacy)

    The PAX format is recommended for new archives as it supports:
    - Unlimited filename length
    - Unlimited file size
    - Unicode filenames
    - Extended attributes
    - Nanosecond timestamps

    @section tarutilityfunctions Utility Functions

    @code{.py}
# Get human-readable names for TAR constants
string cm_name = get_compression_name(TAR_CM_GZIP);     # "gzip"
string fmt_name = get_format_name(TAR_FORMAT_PAX);       # "pax"
string unknown = get_compression_name(99);               # "unknown"
    @endcode

    @section tarentryinfo Entry Information

    Each entry in a TAR archive has associated metadata:

    @code{.py}
hashdecl Qore::Tar::TarEntryInfo {
    string name;              # Entry path/name
    int size;                 # Uncompressed size
    date modified;            # Modification time
    date accessed;            # Access time (if available)
    date created;             # Creation time (if available)
    int mode;                 # Unix permissions
    int uid;                  # User ID
    int gid;                  # Group ID
    *string uname;            # User name
    *string gname;            # Group name
    string type;              # "file", "directory", "symlink", "hardlink", etc.
    *string link_target;      # Target for symlinks/hardlinks
    bool is_directory;        # Convenience flag
    bool is_symlink;          # Convenience flag
    bool is_hardlink;         # Convenience flag
    *int devmajor;            # Device major number (for device nodes)
    *int devminor;            # Device minor number (for device nodes)
}
    @endcode

    @section tardataprovider Data Provider API

    The \c TarDataProvider module provides integration with Qore's data provider framework.
    See the TarDataProvider module documentation for details:
    @htmlonly
    <a href="../../TarDataProvider/html/index.html">TarDataProvider module documentation</a>
    @endhtmlonly

    @code{.py}
%requires TarDataProvider

# Get the data provider factory
AbstractDataProviderFactory factory = DataProvider::getFactoryEx("tar");
AbstractDataProvider provider = factory.create();

# Navigate to the create-archive action
AbstractDataProvider createAction = provider.getChildProviderEx("archive").getChildProviderEx("create");

# Or use the shorthand path navigation
AbstractDataProvider dp = DataProvider::getFactoryObjectFromStringEx("tar{}/archive/create");
    @endcode

    @subsection tardpactions Available Data Provider Actions

    |!Path|!Action|!Description
    |/archive/create|create-archive|Create a new tar archive
    |/archive/extract|extract-archive|Extract files with optional include/exclude filters
    |/archive/list|list-archive|List archive contents with metadata
    |/archive/info|get-archive-info|Get archive metadata and statistics
    |/archive/add|add-files|Add files to an existing archive
    |/archive/verify|verify-archive|Verify archive integrity without extracting
    |/archive/encrypt|encrypt-archive|Encrypt archive with AES-256-CBC
    |/archive/decrypt|decrypt-archive|Decrypt an AES-256-CBC encrypted archive
    |/archive/split|split-archive|Split archive into fixed-size chunks
    |/archive/join|join-archive|Join chunks back into single archive
    |/file/extract|extract-file|Extract a single file from archive
    |/file/read|read-file|Read file content without extracting to disk
    |/data/compress|compress-data|Compress data to tar format (in-memory)
    |/data/decompress|decompress-data|Decompress tar data (in-memory)
    |/directory/archive|archive-directory|Archive a directory tree recursively
    |/stream/extract|stream-extract|Stream extract large files
    |/entry/delete|delete-entry|Delete entry from archive

    @subsection tardpexamples Data Provider Examples

    @code{.py}
%requires TarDataProvider
%requires DataProvider

auto provider = DataProvider::getFactory("tar").create();

# Create an archive with files
auto createProvider = provider.getChildProvider("archive").getChildProvider("create");
hash<auto> result = createProvider.doRequest({
    "archive_path": "/tmp/backup.tar.gz",
    "compression": "gzip",
    "files": (
        {"name": "readme.txt", "text": "Hello World"},
        {"name": "data.bin", "data": binary("binary content")},
    ),
});

# Extract with filters
auto extractProvider = provider.getChildProvider("archive").getChildProvider("extract");
hash<auto> ext_result = extractProvider.doRequest({
    "archive_path": "/tmp/backup.tar.gz",
    "destination": "/tmp/output",
    "include": ("*.txt",),
    "overwrite": True,
});
printf("Extracted %d entries\n", ext_result.extracted_count);

# Encrypt an archive
auto encryptProvider = provider.getChildProvider("archive").getChildProvider("encrypt");
hash<auto> enc_result = encryptProvider.doRequest({
    "archive_path": "/tmp/backup.tar.gz",
    "key": get_random_bytes(32),
});
# enc_result.encrypted_data contains [IV + ciphertext]

# Split into 1MB chunks
auto splitProvider = provider.getChildProvider("archive").getChildProvider("split");
hash<auto> split_result = splitProvider.doRequest({
    "archive_path": "/tmp/backup.tar.gz",
    "chunk_size": 1048576,
});
printf("Split into %d chunks\n", split_result.chunk_count);
    @endcode

    @section tarqtar qtar Command-Line Tool

    The module includes \c qtar, a command-line tool for TAR archive operations:

    @verbatim
qtar - Qore TAR archive tool

Commands:
  c  create   Create a new archive
  x  extract  Extract archive contents
  t  list     List archive contents
  a  add      Add files to an existing archive
  i  info     Show archive metadata
  e  encrypt  Encrypt an archive (AES-256-CBC)
  d  decrypt  Decrypt an encrypted archive
     cat      Read a single entry to stdout
     verify   Verify archive integrity
     diff     Compare archive vs filesystem
    @endverbatim

    @subsection tarqtarexamples qtar Examples

    @code{.sh}
# Create a gzip-compressed archive (compression auto-detected from extension)
qtar create backup.tar.gz /path/to/project

# Extract with glob filters
qtar extract backup.tar.gz -C /tmp/output --include='*.txt' --exclude='tmp/*'

# Extract with path stripping
qtar extract project.tar.gz -C /output --strip=1

# List contents in long format
qtar list -l archive.tar

# Read a single file to stdout
qtar cat archive.tar.gz path/to/file.txt > output.txt

# Show archive metadata
qtar info archive.tar.gz

# Encrypt/decrypt
qtar encrypt archive.tar archive.tar.enc --gen-key
qtar decrypt archive.tar.enc archive.tar -k <hex-key>

# Verify archive integrity
qtar verify backup.tar.gz

# Compare archive against filesystem
qtar diff backup.tar.gz /path/to/project
    @endcode

    @section tarerrors Error Handling

    All TAR operations throw exceptions of type \c "TAR-ERROR" when errors occur:

    @code{.py}
try {
    TarFile tar("nonexistent.tar", "r");
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    if (ex.err == "TAR-ERROR") {
        printf("TAR error: %s\n", ex.desc);
    }
}
    @endcode

    Path traversal attacks are detected and rejected with \c "TAR-SECURITY-ERROR":

    @code{.py}
# Archives containing paths like "../../../etc/passwd" are rejected
try {
    tar.extractAll(<TarExtractOptions>{"destination": "/safe/dir"});
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    if (ex.err == "TAR-SECURITY-ERROR") {
        printf("Blocked path traversal: %s\n", ex.desc);
    }
}
    @endcode

    @section tarreleasenotes Release Notes

    @subsection tar_1_1 tar Module Version 1.1

    <b>New Features:</b>
    - Include/exclude glob-style filters for @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::extractAll() "extractAll()"
      (\c include, \c exclude fields in @ref Qore::Tar::TarExtractOptions "TarExtractOptions")
    - Path component stripping during extraction (\c strip_count in
      @ref Qore::Tar::TarExtractOptions "TarExtractOptions")
    - Progress callbacks for @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::extractAll() "extractAll()" with
      \c code parameter overload
    - Stream-based constructors: @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::constructor(Qore::InputStream) "TarFile(InputStream)"
      and @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::constructor(Qore::OutputStream, *hash<Qore::Tar::TarCreateOptions>) "TarFile(OutputStream)"
    - @ref Qore::Tar::get_compression_name() "get_compression_name()" and
      @ref Qore::Tar::get_format_name() "get_format_name()" utility functions
    - @ref Qore::Tar::TarFile::extractAll() "extractAll()" returns list of extracted entry names
    - \c qtar command-line tool with create, extract, list, add, info, cat, encrypt, decrypt,
      verify, and diff commands

    <b>Data Provider Improvements:</b>
    - All action registrations include \c options and \c output_type for full UI support
    - \c AllowedValueInfo with \c display_name for all enumerated values
    - New providers: TarVerifyArchiveDataProvider, TarArchiveDirectoryDataProvider,
      TarStreamExtractDataProvider, TarDeleteEntryDataProvider,
      TarEncryptArchiveDataProvider, TarDecryptArchiveDataProvider,
      TarSplitArchiveDataProvider, TarJoinArchiveDataProvider

    <b>Bug Fixes:</b>
    - Fixed use-after-free in extractAll() where entry_name pointer was used after
      archive_entry_set_pathname() invalidated the libarchive internal buffer
    - Removed non-functional fields from TarAddOptions (compression_method,
      compression_level, format were declared but never parsed)
    - Removed non-functional strip_components bool from TarExtractOptions
      (replaced by working strip_count int)

    @subsection tar_1_0 tar Module Version 1.0
    - Initial release
    - Multiple compression methods (gzip, bzip2, xz, zstd, lz4)
    - Multiple TAR formats (USTAR, PAX, GNU, V7)
    - Streaming API for large files
    - In-memory archive operations
    - Symlink and hardlink support
    - Data provider module with registered actions

